{{Short description|Representation of music in film or other art forms}}
{{distinguish|text=[[Graphical sound]], a recording technique}}
{{redirects|Color music|the suite by Michael Torke|Color Music|"color" and "coloration"|Color (medieval music)}}

[[File:Lichtspiel Opus I (1921).webm|thumb|thumbtime=250|''Lichtspiel: Opus I'' (1921) by [[Walter Ruttmann]]]]
'''Visual music''', sometimes called '''color music''', refers to the creation of a visual analogue to musical form by adapting musical structures for visual composition, which can also include [[silent film]]s or silent [[Lumia (art)|Lumia]] work. It also refers to methods or devices which can translate sounds or music into a related visual presentation. An expanded definition may include the translation of music to painting; this was the original definition of the term, as coined by [[Roger Fry]] in 1912 to describe the work of [[Wassily Kandinsky]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/art/3653012/The-man-who-heard-his-paintbox-hiss.html|title=The man who heard his paintbox hiss|first=Ossian|last=Ward|date=9 June 2006|via=www.telegraph.co.uk}}</ref> There are a variety of definitions of visual music, particularly as the field continues to expand. In some recent writing, usually in the [[fine art]] world, visual music is often conflated with or defined as [[synaesthesia]], though historically this has never been a definition of visual music. Visual music has also been defined as a form of [[intermedia]].

Visual music also refers to systems which convert music or sound directly into visual forms, such as [[film]], [[video]], [[computer graphics]], installations or performances by means of a mechanical instrument, an artist's interpretation, or a computer. The reverse is applicable also, literally converting images to sound by drawn objects and figures on a film's soundtrack, in a technique known as drawn or [[graphical sound]]. Famous visual music artists include [[Mary Ellen Bute]], [[Jordan Belson]], [[Oskar Fischinger]], [[Norman McLaren]], [[John Whitney Sr.]], and [[Thomas Wilfred]], plus a number of contemporary artists.

{{Short description|Czech poet, playwright, and artist (1914–2002)}}
{{More citations needed|date=July 2014}}

[[File:Jiří Kolář 1979 foto Hana Hamplová.jpg|thumb|Kolář in 1979]]

'''Jiří Kolář''' ({{Audio|Jiri kolar.ogg|Czech pronunciation}}; 24 September 1914 – 11 August 2002) was a [[Czechs|Czech]] poet, writer, painter and translator. His work included both literary and visual art.

{{short description|Repetition of similar events in history}}
{{essay-like|date=September 2024}}
{{use dmy dates|date=October 2024}}

'''Historical recurrence''' is the concept that, in the [[study of history]], similar events are sometimes found to re-occur. The concept has been applied to various levels of [[human history]], from the rise and fall of [[empire]]s, to patterns in the history of a given [[polity]], to any two specific events which bear a striking similarity.<ref>G. W. Trompf, ''The Idea of Historical Recurrence in Western Thought, from Antiquity to the Reformation'', Berkeley, University of California Press, 1979, {{ISBN| 0-520-03479-1}}.</ref>

In his 1979 book ''The Idea of Historical Recurrence in Western Thought'', [[G. W. Trompf]] traces historically recurring patterns of political thought and behavior in the West since antiquity.

